<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Douglas (governor)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1803">1803</a>–<a href="/years/1877">1877</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sir James Douglas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Douglas was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a> fur trader and chief factor who founded Fort Victoria in 1843. He governed the Colony of Vancouver Island from 1851 to 1864, concluding the Douglas Treaties with First Nations, and in 1858 also became governor of the new Colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fraser-river">Fraser River</a> gold rush, cutting his ties with the Company. He is often called the father of British Columbia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-douglas-governor.jpg" alt="James Douglas (governor)" width="320" height="377" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_James_Douglas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
